    "id": "news-1789821989622-0",
    "title": "US Grants One-Year Extension for UN Support in Somalia as Security Transition Looms",
    "original_title": "Somalia’s security clock is ticking: Can Somali forces survive the UNSOS, AUSSOM drawdown?",
    "slug": "us-grants-one-year-extension-for-un-support-in-somalia-as-security-transition-looms",
    "summary": "The United States has extended the UN's timeline for phasing out support to Somali security forces by one year, but experts warn that the transition remains precarious as Al-Shabaab threats persist.",
    "content": "The United States has agreed to give the United Nations an additional year to gradually reduce its logistical and operational support for African Union peacekeepers in Somalia. The decision, announced on 18 September 2026, delays the full drawdown of the UN Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS) and the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), which were originally scheduled to conclude earlier. The extension provides temporary relief but does not resolve the underlying uncertainty about Somalia's ability to manage its own security.\n\nThe transition has been a long-standing concern. For nearly two decades, the African Union Mission in Somalia (AMISOM) and its successor ATMIS have been pivotal in protecting the Somali government and key population centers from Al-Shabaab. The UN Support Office in Somalia (UNSOS) has provided critical logistics, medical evacuations, and other support to these forces. The drawdown process began with the aim of handing over security responsibilities to the Somali National Army (SNA) by the end of 2024, but persistent challenges, including corruption, inadequate training, and insufficient equipment, have delayed progress. The International Crisis Group has repeatedly warned that premature withdrawal could create a security vacuum.\n\nThe United States' decision to grant more time reflects ongoing international concern about Somalia's stability. Key figures involved include Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, who has advocated for a slower transition, and US officials who have pushed for greater accountability and efficiency in the use of international funds. The extended timeline allows for phased reductions but does not guarantee additional resources or a permanent solution. The Somali government has been working to build up its forces, but recent Al-Shabaab attacks, including a deadly siege in Mogadishu in 2025, underscore the group's resilience.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the security situation in the homeland is of paramount importance. Many diaspora members have invested in businesses, properties, and family support in Somalia, and a resurgence of Al-Shabaab could threaten these investments and the safety of relatives. Additionally, the diaspora plays a crucial role in funding and advocating for development projects. A stable Somalia is essential for their continued engagement, and any perception of a security decline could deter return and investment. The diaspora also closely follows international decisions on Somalia, as these signal the level of global commitment to the country's future.\n\nThe coming year will be critical. Somali forces must demonstrate their ability to take over security responsibilities, while international partners must ensure that the transition does not compromise the gains made against Al-Shabaab. The extension offers a window of opportunity, but without sustained support and genuine progress, the security clock will continue ticking.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-security-transition-unsos-aussom-somali-forces-al-shabaab/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/somalia-security-transition-unsos-aussom-somali-forces-al-shabaab/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Security",
    "published_at": "2026-09-19T09:53:06.000Z",
